# Copy to `apps/web/.env.local` and you are done:
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env.local
#
# **This file lives in `apps/web`, not at the repo root, because that is where
# Next reads it from.** `next dev` loads `.env.local` from its own project
# directory; a root one would be ignored in silence, which is the worst way for
# a configuration file to be wrong.
#
# **Development-tier values only, and that is a rule rather than a habit.**
# `turbo.json` declares `.env*` a `build` input, so anything written here is
# hashed into the build cache and travels with a remote-cached artifact. A
# production URL in this file is one `git add` from an incident (spec 0002, C13).
# Real credentials live in `fly secrets` — `docs/policy/security.md` ->
# `secret-store`.
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored. This file is committed, and holds no secret.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The database. `pnpm db:up` starts what these point at; see `docker-compose.yaml`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pooled. What the application uses, through PgBouncer in transaction-pooling
# mode — the same mode PlanetScale's pooler runs in (spec 0002, DD2). Session
# state does not survive between transactions on this connection, and that is
# the point of it being here rather than pointing straight at 5432.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://recomencemos:recomencemos@localhost:6432/recomencemos

# Direct. Migrations only. DDL, long transactions and anything session-scoped
# cannot run through a transaction pooler, so `@repo/domain/migrate` takes this
# one and the runtime client takes the pooled one above.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://recomencemos:recomencemos@localhost:5432/recomencemos

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notifications. `@repo/notifications` is the seam; two transports implement it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Which implementation of the seam runs.** `terminal` or `resend`.
#
# `terminal` is the development inbox: instead of delivering, it prints the
# notification — the magic link first, on a line of its own — to the terminal
# running `pnpm dev`, so you copy the link from there and never need a real
# mailbox. It is a second transport rather than a mode, which is why no call
# site knows it exists.
#
# **It refuses to exist outside `development` and `test`**, so it cannot end up
# on a deploy quietly swallowing everyone's mail.
#
# There is no default and an unset value throws. Neither answer is safe in both
# places: `resend` by default mails a stranger from an unwarmed domain on your
# first sign-in loop, and `terminal` by default makes a real deploy send nothing
# at all. Production sets `resend` through `fly secrets`.
NOTIFICATIONS_TRANSPORT=terminal

# **The kill switch.** Set it to anything and every send is refused, logged, and
# never delivered — one variable and a restart rather than a deploy, because a
# send is the one irreversible act in this system (DD10, DD14).
#
# Empty, `off`, `false`, `0` and `no` leave sending on. *Everything else engages
# it*, a typo included: the failure worth designing against is a switch someone
# believes is on and isn't.
#
# It is `off` here because the line above already makes development safe — with
# the terminal transport there is nothing to be irreversible about, and a
# fresh clone should be able to run the sign-in loop end to end. Engage it when
# you point a development machine at `resend`.
NOTIFICATIONS_KILL_SWITCH=off

# Who the mail is from.
#
# **This is the real, verified sending subdomain**, not a placeholder — Resend is
# configured for `mail.recomencemos.online` and its SPF, DKIM and DMARC records
# resolve (runbook §4). It is written here rather than left as `example.test`
# because neither half is a secret, and because §4 wants every send a developer
# makes to count toward the domain's warm-up curve instead of going nowhere.
# `RESEND_API_KEY` is still absent from this file and must stay absent.
#
# **Not `noreply@`**, which is still DD14's rule: the local part names someone.
# There is no `NOTIFICATIONS_REPLY_TO` beside it, and that is the amendment
# rather than an omission — the subdomain is send-only in Resend, so it publishes
# no MX record and nothing receives. A `Reply-To` pointing at a mailbox that does
# not exist is a dead end asserted; without one, a reply bounces and she is told.
# The variable returns the day a mailbox does.
#
# `senderIdentity()` throws rather than invent a value, and the `terminal`
# transport never reads this at all.
NOTIFICATIONS_FROM=Recomencemos <hola@mail.recomencemos.online>

# `RESEND_API_KEY` is deliberately **absent from this file, and must stay absent**.
# It is a credential, so it lives in `fly secrets` and in the operator's password
# manager (runbook §1, NFR24) — and `turbo.json` declares `.env*` a `build` input,
# so a key written here is hashed into the build cache and travels with a
# remote-cached artifact. To send from a development machine, put it in
# `.env.local`, which is gitignored.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication. `@repo/domain` owns Better Auth and its tables (spec 0002, DD5).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Where this app is.** Better Auth builds the magic-link URL from it, and
# `trustedOrigins` derives from it, so a wrong value produces links that go
# nowhere and a CSRF check that refuses the app's own forms. Not a credential —
# it is `globalPassThroughEnv` in `turbo.json` rather than absent from it.
#
# **Under `pnpm dev` this is not the origin the app runs at, and that is correct.**
# The dev server is served by name rather than by port (ADR-0018) and the name
# carries the git branch, so no static value here could be right for every
# worktree at once. `authBaseUrl` in `@repo/domain` prefers `PORTLESS_URL` — the
# origin the proxy is actually serving — whenever `NODE_ENV` is not `production`.
# So leave this line alone; a hostname you saw on the banner does not belong in
# it. It is what stands under `PORTLESS=0 pnpm dev`, and what production sets
# through `fly secrets`.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# **The signing secret**, and the one value in this file that is deliberately a
# real-looking one rather than a placeholder.
#
# A fresh clone's first act is signing in, and a variable you have to generate
# before that works is a wall at step one. So a development value is committed
# here — and what makes that safe is a mechanism rather than a note: `authSecret`
# in `@repo/domain` **refuses this exact string when `NODE_ENV=production`**, so
# it cannot reach a deploy quietly. Better Auth rejects its own placeholder
# secrets the same way; this is that idea applied to ours, because Better Auth
# has never heard of this string.
#
# Production sets its own through `fly secrets set` (NFR24, runbook §1):
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Rotating it is an operational event, not hygiene: it will also encrypt the
# Admin's TOTP secrets and backup codes at rest, so a rotation invalidates every
# second factor and needs the recovery step in DD5 beside it.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=development-only-secret-do-not-deploy-3f9a2c

# `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` are deliberately **absent from
# this file, and must stay absent** — NFR24 names them as runtime credentials, so
# they live in `fly secrets` and in the operator's password manager, and
# `turbo.json` declares `.env*` a `build` input.
#
# **The sign-in page works without them**, which is the point of them being
# optional rather than required: with neither set, the Google door is *absent*
# rather than broken, and the email door is the whole page. That is also the
# state CI runs in. To try the Google door locally, put both in `.env.local`,
# which is gitignored, and add `http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google`
# as an authorised redirect URI on the Google Cloud OAuth client.
#
# **That door is the one surface the dev proxy cannot serve**, so run
# `PORTLESS=0 pnpm dev` for the session that tests it. Google will not accept a
# `.localhost` HTTPS redirect URI, and the proxied hostname carries the branch
# anyway — it would need registering per worktree. `PORTLESS=0` puts the app back
# on `http://localhost:3000`, which is the URI registered above. ADR-0018 records
# why this bypass is the accepted answer rather than a gap.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The *responsable del tratamiento*. Ley 1581 makes the operator personally
# answerable for this data (intent Q4), and the *aviso de privacidad* at
# `/privacy` has to name them and a mailbox that answers a *consulta* or a
# *reclamo*.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# **Deployment configuration rather than committed copy.** A legal name and a
# mailbox that answers a reclamo are one person's real identity, and a public
# repository is not where they belong. Neither value is a credential, so both
# are `globalPassThroughEnv` in `turbo.json` rather than absent from it — the
# same treatment `NOTIFICATIONS_FROM` and `BETTER_AUTH_URL` get.
#
# **These two strings are placeholders and are recognised as such.**
# `responsibleParty()` refuses them when `NODE_ENV=production`, exactly as
# `authSecret` refuses the development secret above, so a deploy cannot go live
# with a privacy notice naming nobody. In development they are what `/privacy`
# renders, which is what lets a fresh clone read the page without anyone's real
# name being in git.
#
# Production sets both through `fly secrets` (runbook §7).
RESPONSIBLE_PARTY_NAME=Responsable de desarrollo
RESPONSIBLE_PARTY_EMAIL=datos@localhost

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Recorded proof. `pnpm ui-proof` publishes a Build session's before/after
# capture to an object store and links it from the pull request body.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The five `UI_PROOF_S3_*` and `UI_PROOF_PUBLIC_BASE` values are deliberately
# **absent from this file, and must stay absent**. Two of them are a credential,
# and the other three name one operator's bucket rather than this product's.
#
# They are not deployment configuration and there is no `fly secret` for them:
# a capture is taken on a developer's machine and published from it, so CI never
# sees one and never uploads. `.github/workflows/ui-proof-expire.yml` rewrites a
# line in a pull request body and runs on `GITHUB_TOKEN` alone.
#
# So they live in **`.env.local`, which is gitignored** — `pnpm ui-proof` passes
# `--env-file-if-exists` at it, the same way `db:migrate` and `admin:enrol` do —
# or in the shell, which is the route a worktree needs because a fresh one has no
# `.env.local` at all. Where both are set the shell wins: Node's env file is a
# fallback, not an override.
#
# `pnpm ui-proof:setup` is the wizard that produces all five, and
# `docs/runbooks/ui-proof-artifacts.md` is what it works through. Until it has
# been run, `pnpm ui-proof publish --pr <n> --dry-run` is the only form that
# works — and it needs none of these.
